  "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={sin}^{3}\u2061({x}^{2}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}\u2061({y}^{2})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({sin}^{3}\u2061({y}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3{sin}^{2}\u2061({y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6y{sin}^{2}\u2061({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6y{sin}^{2}\u2061({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}\u2061({y}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}\u2061({y}^{2})}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}\u2061({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}\u2061({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}\u2061({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}\u2061({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
